> I have started a plan to build an abstraction around model and tool selection in my AI system. Here is the contract for tools:
> """
> Tool protocol and normalized typed tool-result contract.
> 
> Tools are executable capabilities. In the initial synchronous system they may
> be invoked directly by a responder. In the workflow system they are executed
> as Operations and yield Artifacts. Tool interfaces should therefore avoid
> assuming that their primary result is user-visible text.
> 
> Expected tool callable shape:
> 
> ToolCallable = Callable[
>     [ToolInvocation],
>     ToolResult,
> ]
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class ToolInvocation:
>     operation_id: str | None
>     conversation_id: str
>     arguments: Mapping[str, Any]
>     config: Mapping[str, Any]
>     context: PlannerContext | None
>     provenance_requirements: Mapping[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Tool:
>     name: str
>     description: str
>     fn: ToolCallable | Callable[..., Any]
>     config: ToolConfig
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
>     version: str = "1"
> 
> Compatibility:
> - registry.py may accept legacy functions such as
>   `fn(prompt, config) -> str`.
> - tools/base.py normalizes such outputs to ToolResult/Artifact.
> - Tool configuration defaults and allowed discrete options are validated here.
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py or workflow/workers.py invokes:
>       result = tool.fn(invocation)
> - the caller persists raw temporary output as appropriate, converts relevant
>   materials to artifacts/source excerpts, and never blindly injects untrusted
>   tool text into system instructions.
> """
> 
> Now, here is the documentation for a tool available through OpenAI:
> ## File search
> 
> `file_search` runs semantic and keyword search over files you have uploaded to one or more vector stores, returning a `file_search_call` item and `file_citation` annotations. You must create the vector store and add files first (`client.vector_stores.create`, then `client.vector_stores.files.create`).
> 
> ```python
> resp = client.responses.create(
>     model="gpt-6-astra",
>     input="What do our IAM runs assume for the discount rate?",
>     tools=[{
>         "type": "file_search",
>         "vector_store_ids": ["vs_abc123"],
>         "max_num_results": 5,
>         "filters": {"type": "in", "key": "category", "value": ["paper", "appendix"]},
>     }],
>     include=["file_search_call.results"],
> )
> ```
> 
> | Option | Values / range | Notes |
> | --- | --- | --- |
> | `vector_store_ids` | list of IDs (required) | Stores to search |
> | `max_num_results` | integer | Fewer results = lower cost and latency, possibly lower quality |
> | `filters` | comparison filter on file attributes (e.g. `type: "in"`, `key`, `value`) | Attributes are set per file; full operator list is in the [retrieval guide](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/retrieval) |
> 
> Search results are not returned by default; add `include: ["file_search_call.results"]` to see them. Supported formats include PDF, DOCX, PPTX, MD, TXT, TEX, HTML, JSON and common source-code files. Rate limits run 100 to 1,000 requests per minute depending on tier.
> 
> Can you construct a full tool implementation for this?
